== THE THIRD STAGE

The Interpreter then called for one of his servants, a man named Great-heart, and told him to take a sword, and helmet, and shield; and, Take these daughters of mine, he said, and lead them to the house called Beautiful, where they will rest next. So he took his weapons and went before them; and the Interpreter said, God speed. Those who belonged to the household also sent them off with many a good wish. So they went on their way, and sang,

#quote[This place hath been our second stage: \
Here we have heard, and seen \
Those good things, that from age to age \
To others hid have been.]

#quote[The dunghill-raker, spider, hen, \
The chicken, too, to me \
Have taught a lesson: let me then \
Conformed to it be.]

#quote[The butcher, garden, and the field, \
The robin and his bait, \
Also the rotten tree, doth yield \
Me argument of weight,]

#quote[To move me for to watch and pray, \
To strive to be sincere; \
To take my cross up day by day, \
And serve the Lord with fear.]

Now I saw in my dream that they went on, with Great-Heart in front of them. So they went on, and came to the place where Christian’s burden fell off his back and tumbled into a sepulcher. Here they stopped for a while, and here they also blessed God. Now, said Christiana, I remember what we were told at the gate, namely, that we would have pardon by word and deed: by word, that is, by the promise; by deed, that is, in the way it was obtained. What the promise is, I know something of; but what it is to have pardon by deed, or in the way it was obtained, Mr. Great-Heart, I suppose you know; so, if you please, let us hear you speak about it.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Pardon by the deed done is pardon obtained by someone for another who needs it; not by the person pardoned, but in the way, says another, in which I have obtained it. So then, to speak to the question more fully, the pardon that you, and Mercy, and these boys have attained was obtained by another; that is, by him who let you in at the gate. And he has obtained it in this double way: he has performed righteousness to cover you, and shed his blood to wash you in.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: But if he parts with his righteousness to us, what will he have for himself?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: He has more righteousness than you need, or than he needs himself.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Please show us how that can be.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: With all my heart: but first I must lay this down, that he of whom we are now about to speak is one who has no equal. He has two natures in one person, plain to be distinguished, impossible to be divided. To each of these natures a righteousness belongs, and each righteousness is essential to that nature; so that one could as easily make that nature cease to exist as separate its justice or righteousness from it. So we are not made partakers of these righteousnesses in such a way that they, or any of them, should be put on us so that we might be made just and live by them. Besides these, there is a righteousness which this person has as these two natures are joined in one. And this is not the righteousness of the Godhead, as distinguished from the manhood, nor the righteousness of the manhood, as distinguished from the Godhead; but a righteousness which lies in the union of both natures, and may properly be called the righteousness that is essential to his being prepared by God and fitted for the mediatory office with which he was to be entrusted. If he parts with his first righteousness, he parts with his Godhead; if he parts with his second righteousness, he parts with the purity of his manhood; if he parts with his third, he parts with the perfection that fits him for the office of mediation. He has therefore another righteousness, which lies in performance, or obedience to a revealed will; and that is what he puts on sinners, and that is what covers their sins. So he says, “Just as through the disobedience of the one man the many were made sinners, so also through the obedience of the one man the many will be made righteous.” Rom 5:19.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: But are the other righteousnesses of no use to us?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes; for though they are essential to his natures and office, and cannot be imparted to another, yet it is by virtue of them that the righteousness that justifies is efficacious for that purpose. The righteousness of his Godhead gives power to his obedience; the righteousness of his manhood gives his obedience the capacity to justify; and the righteousness that lies in the union of these two natures to his office gives that righteousness authority to do the work for which it was ordained.]

So then here is a righteousness that Christ, as God, has no need of, for he is God without it. Here is a righteousness that Christ, as man, has no need of to make him so, for he is perfect man without it. Again, here is a righteousness that Christ, as God-man, has no need of, for he is perfectly so without it. Here then is a righteousness that Christ, as God and as God-man, has no need of with regard to himself, and therefore he can spare it; a justifying righteousness that he does not need for himself, and therefore he gives it away. That is why it is called the gift of righteousness. Since Christ Jesus the Lord has put himself under the law, this righteousness must be given away; for the law binds the one who is under it not only to do justly, but to practice charity. Rom 5:17. So he must, or ought to by the law, if he has two coats, give one to him who has none. Now, our Lord indeed has two coats, one for himself, and one to spare; so he freely gives one to those who have none. And so, Christiana and Mercy, and the rest of you who are here, your pardon comes by deed, or by the work of another man. Your Lord Christ is the one who worked, and he has given away what he worked for to the next poor beggar he meets.

But again, for pardon by deed, something must be paid to God as a price, as well as something prepared to cover us with. Sin has handed us over to the just curse of a righteous law: now from this curse we must be justified by way of redemption, a price being paid for the harm we have done; and this is by the blood of your Lord, who came and stood in your place and stead, and died your death for your transgressions. In this way he has ransomed you from your transgressions by blood, and covered your polluted and deformed souls with righteousness, Rom 8:34; and for the sake of this, God passes over you and will not hurt you when he comes to judge the world. Gal 3:13.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: This is splendid! Now I see that there was something to be learned from our being pardoned by word and deed. Good Mercy, let us work hard to keep this in mind; and, my children, you remember it too. But, sir, was it not this that made my good Christian’s burden fall from his shoulder, and made him give three leaps for joy?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes, it was believing this that cut the strings that could not be cut by any other means; and it was to give him proof of the power of this that he was allowed to carry his burden to the cross.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: I thought so; for though my heart was light and joyful before, it is ten times more light and joyful now. And I am persuaded by what I have felt, though I have felt only a little as yet, that if the most burdened man in the world were here, and saw and believed as I now do, it would make his heart all the more merry and glad.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: The sight and consideration of these things bring us not only comfort and the easing of a burden, but also a tender affection, born in us by them: for who, if he thinks even once that pardon comes not only by promise but in this way, can help being moved by the way and means of his redemption, and so by the man who has worked it out for him?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: True; I think it makes my heart bleed to think that he should bleed for me. Oh, you loving One! Oh, you blessed One! You deserve to have me; you have bought me. You deserve to have all of me; you have paid for me ten thousand times more than I am worth. No wonder this made the tears stand in my husband’s eyes, and made him trudge on so nimbly. I am sure he wished I were with him; but, vile wretch that I was, I let him come all alone. Oh, Mercy, if only your father and mother were here; yes, and Mrs. Timorous too: yes, I wish now with all my heart that even Madam Wanton were here too. Surely, surely, their hearts would be moved; and neither the fear of the one nor the powerful lusts of the other could persuade them to go home again and refuse to become good pilgrims.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: You speak now in the warmth of your feelings; do you think it will always be like this with you? Besides, this is not given to everyone, nor to everyone who saw your Jesus bleed. There were some who stood by and saw the blood run from his heart to the ground, and yet were so far from this that instead of mourning, they laughed at him, and instead of becoming his disciples, they hardened their hearts against him. So all that you have, my daughters, you have by a special impression made by divine meditation on what I have said to you. Remember that you were told that the hen, by her common call, gives no food to her chickens. So you have this by a special grace.]

Now I saw in my dream that they went on until they came to the place where Simple, and Sloth, and Presumption lay asleep when Christian went by on pilgrimage; and there they were, hanging in chains a little way off on the other side.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Then Mercy said to their guide and conductor, Who are these three men, and why are they hanged there?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: These three were men of very bad character; they had no wish to be pilgrims themselves, and whoever they could, they hindered. They were sloth and folly themselves, and whoever they could persuade they made like themselves, and also taught them to presume that they would come out well at last. They were asleep when Christian went by; and now, as you go by, they are hanged.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: But could they persuade anyone to think as they did?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: Yes, they turned several out of the way. There was Slow-pace, whom they persuaded to do as they did. They also won over one Short-wind, one No-heart, one Linger-after-Lust, and one Sleepy-head, and a young woman whose name was Dull, to turn out of the way and become like them. Besides, they spread a bad report of your Lord, persuading others that he was a hard taskmaster. They also spread an evil report of the good Land, saying it was not half as good as some made out. They also began to slander his servants, and to count the best of them meddling, troublesome busybodies. Further, they would call the bread of God husks; the comforts of his children, fancies; the travel and labor of pilgrims, things to no purpose.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Why, said Christiana, if they were like that, they will never be mourned by me: they have only what they deserve; and I think it is good that they hang so near the highway, so that others may see and take warning. But would it not have been good if their crimes had been engraved on some plate of iron or brass, and left here where they did harm, as a warning to other bad men?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: So they are, as you can see if you go a little way toward the wall.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: No, no; let them hang, and their names rot, and their crimes live forever against them. I think it a great favor that they were hanged before we came here: otherwise who knows what they might have done to poor women like us? Then she turned it into a song, saying,]

#quote[“Now then you three hang there, and be a sign \
To all that shall against the truth combine. \
And let him that comes after, fear this end, \
If unto pilgrims he is not a friend. \
And thou, my soul, of all such men beware, \
That unto holiness opposers are.”]

So they went on till they came to the foot of the hill Difficulty, where again the good Mr. Great-Heart took the opportunity to tell them what happened there when Christian himself went by. So he took them first to the spring. Look, he said, this is the spring that Christian drank from before he went up this hill; and then it was clear and good, but now it is muddied by the feet of some who do not want pilgrims to quench their thirst here. Ezek 34:18, 19. At this Mercy said, And why so envious, I wonder? But, said their guide, it will do, if it is drawn up and put into a vessel that is clean and good; for then the dirt will sink to the bottom, and the water will come out clearer by itself. So this is what Christiana and her companions had to do. They drew it up, and put it into an earthenware pot, and let it stand till the dirt had sunk to the bottom, and then they drank it.

Next he showed them the two by-ways at the foot of the hill, where Formality and Hypocrisy lost themselves. And, he said, these are dangerous paths. Two were lost here when Christian came by; and although, as you see, these ways have since been blocked with chains, posts, and a ditch, yet there are those who will choose to risk going this way rather than take the trouble to go up this hill.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: “The way of transgressors is hard.” Prov 13:15. It is a wonder that they can get into these ways without danger of breaking their necks.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Great-Heart]: They will risk it: yes, if at any time any of the King’s servants happen to see them, and call to them, and tell them that they are on the wrong road, and warn them of the danger, then they rail at them in answer, and say, “As for the word you have spoken to us in the name of the King, we will not listen to you! Instead, we will do everything we vowed to do.” Jer 44:16, 17. Indeed, if you look a little further, you will see that these ways have been marked as a warning plainly enough, not only by these posts, and ditch, and chain, but also by being hedged up: yet they will choose to go that way.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: They are idle; they do not like taking trouble; an uphill road is unpleasant to them. So it is fulfilled in them as it is written, “The way of the slothful is like a hedge of thorns.” Prov 15:19. Yes, they would rather choose to walk on a snare than go up this hill, and the rest of this way to the city.]

Then they set off, and began to go up the hill, and up the hill they went. But before they got to the top, Christiana began to pant, and said, I dare say this hill leaves you out of breath; no wonder if those who love their ease more than their souls choose a smoother way for themselves.

Then Mercy said, I must sit down; and the youngest of the children began to cry. Come, come, said Great-Heart, do not sit down here; for a little higher up is the Prince’s arbor. Then he took the little boy by the hand, and led him up to it.

When they had come to the arbor, they were very glad to sit down, for they were all in a sweltering heat. Then Mercy said, “How sweet is rest to those who labor.” Matt 11:28; and how good the Prince of pilgrims is to provide such resting-places for them! I have heard a great deal about this arbor, but I have never seen it before. But here let us beware of sleeping; for, as I have heard, it cost poor Christian dearly.

Then Mr. Great-Heart said to the little ones, Come, my pretty boys, how are you getting on? What do you think of going on pilgrimage now? Sir, said the youngest, I had almost lost heart; but I thank you for lending me a hand when I needed it. And I remember now what my mother has told me, that the way to heaven is like a ladder, and the way to hell is like going down a hill. But I would rather go up the ladder to life than down the hill to death.

Then Mercy said, But the proverb is, “Going down the hill is easy.” But James said (for that was his name), The day is coming when, in my opinion, going down the hill will be the hardest of all. That’s a good boy, said his master; you have given her the right answer. Then Mercy smiled, but the little boy blushed.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Come, said Christiana, will you eat a bite to sweeten your mouths, while you sit here to rest your legs? for I have here a piece of pomegranate which Mr. Interpreter put into my hand just as I came out of his door; he also gave me a piece of honeycomb, and a little bottle of spirits. I thought he gave you something, said Mercy, because he called you aside. Yes, so he did, said the other; but, said Christiana, it shall still be as I said it should be when we first left home: you shall share in all the good that I have, because you so willingly became my companion. Then she gave to them, and they ate, both Mercy and the boys. And Christiana said to Mr. Great-Heart, Sir, will you do as we do? But he answered, You are going on pilgrimage, and I will soon go back; may what you have do you much good: at home I eat the same every day.]
